# .trivyignore — waivers for Trivy findings you have reviewed and accepted.
#
# Add one CVE ID per line. Comments above the ID are part of the file's git
# history and become your audit trail for why a finding is not being fixed.
#
# Example:
#
#   # CVE-2025-12345 — libcurl in upstream base. Not reachable from our
#   # deployment (container runs behind mTLS ingress, no outbound HTTP).
#   # Revisit 2026-08 when upstream bumps the base image.
#   CVE-2025-12345
#
# Leave this file empty (except for comments) until you actually need a waiver.
# A waiver without a reason is just a mute button, not evidence.

# CVE-2026-22029 — @remix-run/router open-redirect XSS (HIGH, CVSS 8.0).
# Fix lands in @remix-run/router 1.23.2 / react-router 7.11.1+.
# Plausible v3.2.0 pulls 1.18.0 transitively via react-router-dom ^6.25.1
# in assets/dependencies — shipped in the dashboard JS bundle.
#
# Exploit prerequisite: the application must call redirect(unsafeUrl) from
# a react-router loader or action in Data / Framework / RSC mode, with
# the URL attacker-controlled. Per the upstream advisory there is no
# impact in Declarative Mode (<BrowserRouter>).
#
# Plausible uses createBrowserRouter + <RouterProvider> (Data Mode), but
# defines routes purely as { path, element } objects. Grep across
# assets/js/ at v3.2.0: zero matches for `loader:`, zero for `redirect(`,
# zero imports of `redirect` from react-router. The vulnerable code path
# is absent in the current dashboard implementation, so the open-redirect
# sink does not exist.
#
# Impact class if the path existed: client-side XSS in the authenticated
# admin's browser at the Plausible origin. With the path absent, no
# realized impact.
#
# Revisit when Plausible upgrades to react-router 7+ or starts using
# loaders/actions, or by 2026-10 — whichever first.
CVE-2026-22029

# CVE-2026-26996, CVE-2026-27903, CVE-2026-27904 — minimatch ReDoS family
# (each HIGH, CVSS 7.5, DoS only).
# Fixes are spread across minimatch branches; floor for all three is 3.1.4.
# Plausible v3.2.0 pulls minimatch 3.1.2 transitively through eslint,
# jest, prettier, stylelint, tsc — all in assets/devDependencies.
#
# Exploit prerequisite for the entire family: the GLOB PATTERN itself
# must be attacker-controlled. A pattern containing many consecutive `*`
# wildcards (-26996), multiple non-adjacent `**` globstars (-27903), or
# nested `*()` extglobs (-27904) compiles to a regex that V8 backtracks
# exponentially on a non-matching input. The matched string being
# attacker-controlled does NOT exploit any of these.
#
# Plausible's invocations feed minimatch only with hardcoded patterns
# defined in the upstream maintainer's source (e.g. `js/**`,
# `**/*.{js,css,ts,tsx}`). minimatch is not present in the runtime image
# (Mix release ships compiled BEAM bytecode only; no node_modules).
# For an attacker to land a malicious pattern they would need
# upstream-source-write privilege — a supply-chain compromise outside
# the scope of these CVEs.
#
# Impact class: process hang in the CI build runner if exploited. Not
# reachable at runtime by any image consumer.
#
# Revisit when Plausible bumps the dev-tool transitive chain (typically
# follows naturally on the next eslint/jest minor), or by 2026-10 —
# whichever first.
CVE-2026-26996
CVE-2026-27903
CVE-2026-27904

# CVE-2026-27606 — rollup arbitrary file write via path traversal
# (HIGH, CVSS 9.8). The 9.8 score reflects worst-case library-API
# exposure; the realised exposure in Plausible is much narrower.
# Fix lands in rollup 4.59.0+. Plausible v3.2.0 pins rollup ^4.41.1
# in tracker/dependencies and invokes it programmatically from
# tracker/compile.js at image build time to bundle the tracker snippet.
#
# Exploit prerequisite: an attacker must control either (a) a CLI named
# input, (b) a manual chunk alias, or (c) a rollup plugin loaded into
# the build. Plausible's compile.js feeds rollup with input paths and
# chunk names that are entirely hardcoded in the upstream source; the
# only plugin loaded is @rollup/plugin-json from the upstream
# package.json. None of these is attacker-controllable without first
# compromising the upstream Plausible repo (a supply-chain class
# outside the scope of this CVE).
#
# Even in a hypothetical exploit case the build runs inside an ephemeral
# GitHub-hosted runner with no persistent filesystem and no secrets
# beyond the short-lived GHCR push token scoped to this workflow.
# rollup is NOT present in the runtime image.
#
# Impact class: arbitrary file write within the build container. With
# Plausible's hardcoded inputs the sink is unreachable; with a supply-
# chain compromise the runner is the only blast radius.
#
# Revisit when Plausible bumps rollup to >= 4.59.0 (likely in v3.2.1 or
# v3.3.0 via the project's own Dependabot), or by 2026-10 —
# whichever first.
CVE-2026-27606

# CVE-2026-33671 — picomatch ReDoS via crafted extglob patterns
# (HIGH, CVSS 7.5, DoS only).
# Fixes land in picomatch 4.0.4 / 3.0.2 / 2.3.2.
# Plausible v3.2.0 pulls picomatch 2.3.1 (assets, transitive via the
# eslint ecosystem) and 4.0.3 (tracker, transitive via chokidar and
# rollup). Same exposure shape for both — single waiver covers both rows.
#
# Exploit prerequisite: the GLOB PATTERN passed to picomatch must be
# attacker-controlled. A crafted non-matching extglob like
# `*(*(*(a|b)))` triggers catastrophic V8 regex backtracking. The
# matched string being attacker-controlled does not exploit it.
#
# In Plausible's call sites picomatch is fed only hardcoded patterns
# from the upstream source (chokidar watch globs in compile.js,
# eslint config patterns). picomatch is not present in the runtime
# image.
#
# Impact class: process hang in the CI build runner if exploited
# (requires upstream-source-write privilege to land). Not reachable
# at runtime by image consumers.
#
# Revisit when Plausible bumps the affected transitive chain (next
# chokidar / eslint / rollup minor typically pulls picomatch >= 4.0.4),
# or by 2026-10 — whichever first.
CVE-2026-33671
